;;;; hevc/transform-tables.lisp — GENERATED.  The inverse transform matrices (8.6.4.2).
;;;;
;;;; ONE 32x32 MATRIX SERVES ALL FOUR SIZES.  The 16-point transform is its even rows, the 8-point
;;;; every fourth row, the 4-point every eighth — each truncated to as many columns as it has
;;;; points.  That nesting is not a coincidence to be exploited afterwards; it is what the matrix
;;;; was designed for, so that hardware can implement one transform and get the other three.
;;;;
;;;; The entries are a scaled integer approximation of a DCT-II, not a rounded one: they were
;;;; chosen so that the matrix stays close to orthogonal and every row has nearly the same norm,
;;;; which is what lets a decoder use the same dequantisation scale at every size.
;;;;
;;;; The 4x4 luma intra blocks use a DST-VII instead, because an intra residual grows away from the
;;;; predicted edge rather than being flat — a basis that starts small and ends large fits it
;;;; better than a cosine that starts large.  It is only worth a separate matrix at 4x4.
;;;;
;;;; Extracted mechanically, and checked against properties the specification states apart from the
;;;; numbers: row 0 is all 64, row 16 is all +/-64, and every even row is symmetric about its centre
;;;; while every odd row is antisymmetric.

